            <ArticleTitle>[Experience with montelukast, a leukotriene receptor antagonist, in pediatric patients with asthma].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>133-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Montelukast is a specific antagonist of leukotrienes' receptors and constitutes a therapeutic option in controlling asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate efficacy of montelukast in patients with persistent light asthma, persistent moderate asthma and exercise-induced asthma; to try to reduce or to eliminate doses of inhaled steroid; to reduce the use of short-action beta agonists and to assess its tolerability in pediatric patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">40 patients were studied, 17 female, with an age range from 4 to 11 years and a mean of 7 years. Stratification was made by GINA-established parameters.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Steroid dose could be reduced in 88%, with definitive suspension in 66% of patients. Moreover, an improvement in reducing asthma stratification and the use of rescue drugs were registered. 62% of patients with exercise-induced asthma improved and there was a good tolerance with the same side effects.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
